    "description": "A solar-cell characterization overlays two curves against terminal voltage: the I–V curve, which stays nearly flat at the short-circuit current then rolls off sharply to the open-circuit voltage, and the P–V curve (P = V x I), a hump that peaks at the maximum power point (MPP). The MPP is the single operating point where the panel delivers the most power. Sweeping a variable load from short circuit to open circuit traces both curves in one measurement.",
    "dataNeeded": [
      {
        "name": "Terminal voltage",
        "unit": "V"
      },
      {
        "name": "Current",
        "unit": "A"
      },
      {
        "name": "Power",
        "unit": "W"
      }
    ],
    "whatItTellsYou": "The current-axis intercept gives short-circuit current (Isc), the voltage-axis intercept gives open-circuit voltage (Voc), and the peak of the P–V hump locates the MPP (Vmp, Imp) that a maximum-power-point tracker should hold. A sharp, square knee near the MPP means a high fill factor and an efficient cell, whereas a rounded, sagging knee indicates resistive losses.",
